<DocsPage id="cloud-services">

          <p>
            Deployments, domains with real DNS checks, environment variables, databases and logs, run through the
            Vercel, Netlify, Supabase and AWS accounts that already belong to you. Cloud work is part of the task, not a
            panel you operate afterwards.
          </p>
          <p>
            The agent drives it through one permission-gated tool: it checks which providers are connected, detects the
            project's build settings, prepares a Supabase-backed database or authentication, syncs environment values to
            the linked host, publishes, and lists recent deployments. Every action that creates, changes, or spends asks
            you first.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>A publish that is checked</strong><span>Publishing ends with a real browser load of the deployed URL, and the agent reports the page it found and the console errors it saw rather than assuming the deploy worked.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Failures come with logs</strong><span>The agent reads recent deployment logs from the host, so a broken deploy is diagnosed from the actual build or runtime error.</span></li>
            <li><strong>A database it can create</strong><span>The agent creates a real Supabase project on your own account, asks which organization when there are several, and writes the keys and client into your repository.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Migrations it can apply</strong><span>The <code>.sql</code> migrations in your repository can be applied to the linked database, with a record of which ones already ran, so running again is safe.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Your own accounts</strong><span>Vercel, Netlify, and Supabase work once you authorize them, and AWS reads a profile you already configured for the AWS CLI. The resources stay in your accounts; Vector does not host your app and does not proxy your code.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">
            These cloud actions run in the Vector desktop app, where the provider connections live. The terminal CLI
            runs the same agent, but tells it that this tool is unavailable there rather than half-performing the work.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
